    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>In the mid-1960s, a radical cohort of American sculptors mounted a rigorous assault on the foundational premises of Western sculptural tradition. Led by theorists and practitioners Donald Judd and Robert Morris, and later radicalized by Richard Serra, the movement known as Minimalism rejected the longstanding conviction that three-dimensional art should function as an expressive vehicle for human emotion, transcendent illusion, or metaphysical metaphor. Traditional European sculpture, whether classical bronze or modern welded steel, relied on intricate relational composition&mdash;the deliberate balancing of parts within a hierarchy that drew the spectator&rsquo;s gaze inward toward a self-contained, autonomous core. In his seminal 1965 manifesto &ldquo;Specific Objects,&rdquo; Judd repudiated this compositional balancing act as a lingering remnant of anthropomorphic humanism. Instead, Judd advocated for works that were non-relational, unitary, and industrially fabricated, such as unadorned galvanized iron boxes or cantilevered wall stacks that repeat identical modules without hierarchical climax, insisting that an artwork need not represent anything beyond its own literal material presence.</p>\n<p>By stripping the sculptural object of narrative reference and compositional balance, the Minimalists decisively relocated the aesthetic locus from the interior of the artwork to the phenomenological encounter between the object, the surrounding architectural envelope, and the spectator&rsquo;s body. Influenced by Maurice Merleau-Ponty&rsquo;s <i>Phenomenology of Perception</i>, which asserted that consciousness is fundamentally embodied and spatial, Morris observed that simple geometric forms like cubes and polyhedrons do not withhold hidden secrets. Rather, their perceptual clarity forces the viewer to become keenly aware of the conditions of seeing: how natural light shifts across galvanized iron, how scale alters perception as one approaches, and how the physical body navigates real space in real time. The work ceases to be an idealized icon viewed from a detached, static vantage point; it becomes an activated temporal event.</p>\n<p>This phenomenological confrontation achieved its most visceral and physically intimidating manifestation in the monumental steel sculptures of Richard Serra. Utilizing colossal, unadorned sheets of weatherproof Cor-Ten steel, Serra abandoned the protective pedestal to engage directly with gravity, mass, and architectural containment. Rather than welding elements together into permanent joints, Serra frequently relied on pure gravitational balance and sheer tectonic deadweight, leaning multi-ton plates against one another in calculated precariousness. Walking through the narrow, torqued passages of Serra&rsquo;s curved steel labyrinths, the viewer experiences a profound, disorienting somatic sensation. The towering industrial steel tilts precipitously inward, inducing claustrophobia, precariousness, and spatial vertigo. Here, sculpture does not solicit intellectual contemplation from a safe distance; it acts directly upon the spectator&rsquo;s physical equilibrium, compelling a heightened awareness of human vulnerability against the sheer indifference of raw tectonic weight.</p>\n<p>This radical redefinition of the artistic encounter provoked fierce ideological backlash, most famously articulated in Michael Fried&rsquo;s 1967 polemic &ldquo;Art and Objecthood.&rdquo; Fried condemned Minimalism as &ldquo;literalist&rdquo; art and accused it of degenerating into mere &ldquo;theatrics.&rdquo; For Fried, authentic modernist art possessed an instantaneous, self-sufficient quality&mdash;what he celebrated as &ldquo;presentness&rdquo;&mdash;which allowed the viewer to transcend worldly duration in an epiphany of pure visual absorption. Minimalism, Fried charged, corrupted this purity by making the artwork&rsquo;s aesthetic meaning contingent upon the durational presence and physical wandering of an audience, reducing art to a staged situation. Yet what Fried disparaged as theatricality was precisely the Minimalists&rsquo; most enduring theoretical breakthrough: the recognition that meaning does not reside in immutable, pristine essences, but is dynamically constructed through the embodied, contingent choreography between the human subject and the material world.</p>",
