    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>Throughout the eighteenth century, a peculiar architectural phenomenon gripped the landed aristocracy of Great Britain: the deliberate construction of artificial ruins. Across the undulating expanses of newly contoured country estates, wealthy patrons commissioned renowned landscape architects such as William Kent and Capability Brown to erect crumbling Gothic abbeys, fractured classical colonnades, and moss-encrusted sham castles that had never known an authentic past. This vogue for the picturesque folly marked a decisive philosophical break from the seventeenth-century continental garden tradition exemplified by Versailles. Whereas the Cartesian geometry, clipped topiary, and radial avenues of the French formal garden celebrated humanity&rsquo;s absolute rational dominion over subjugated nature, the English landscape garden embraced asymmetric vistas, serpentine lakes, and deliberately weathered masonry. Heavily influenced by the atmospheric Italian landscape paintings of Claude Lorrain and Nicolas Poussin admired during aristocratic Grand Tours, patrons sought to materialize idealized classical canvases upon their own domestic soil. Nature was no longer to be conquered; it was to be aestheticized as a stage for contemplative melancholy.</p>\n<p>At the heart of this ruin aesthetic was the cultivation of a refined, philosophical sensibility known to contemporaries as <i>sensibility</i> or Romantic melancholy. Unlike vulgar depression, eighteenth-century melancholy was considered an elevated intellectual virtue&mdash;a bittersweet meditation on the relentless transience of human endeavor, the vanity of worldly ambition, and the inexorable passage of time. Fractured arches and ivy-draped pediments functioned as tangible <i>memento mori</i>, confronting the viewer with the ultimate fate awaiting all empires and architectural monuments. Closely aligned with Edmund Burke&rsquo;s contemporaneous treatise on the sublime, the sight of colossal stone collapsing under creeping vegetation provoked a pleasurable frisson of terror and solemn awe. In an era intoxicated by Enlightenment doctrines of unbounded historical progress, the ruin served as a poetic brake on hubris, whispering that even the grandest civilizational achievements would eventually be reclaimed by the silent creeping of roots, lichen, and earth.</p>\n<p>Yet this aesthetic obsession contained a profound ontological paradox. To evoke the genuine aura of antiquity, eighteenth-century aristocrats spent exorbitant fortunes manufacturing instantaneous decay. Architects developed elaborate techniques to artificially antique new construction, blasting fresh stone with acid, chipping sharp chisel marks, and importing parasitic weeds to simulate centuries of biological erosion. Prominent connoisseurs like Horace Walpole and later the architect Sir John Soane went so far as to commission paintings of their own residences depicted as future ruins, indulging in a preemptive nostalgia for their own demise. The authentic historical artifact was thus displaced by an engineered simulacrum of catastrophe, transforming genuine temporal decay into an exclusive commodity tailored for polite aristocratic diversion.</p>\n<p>Beneath its philosophical veneer, the aesthetics of ruins served a subtle ideological function in consolidating class hegemony. The creation of vast picturesque estates required the ruthless enforcement of the Parliamentary Enclosure Acts, which violently dispossessed commoners, extinguished customary grazing rights, and depopulated entire rural villages. Often, landscape designers demolished active peasant cottages that marred the picturesque vistas, replacing living agricultural labor with decorative, artificial hermitages and pseudo-historical abbeys. Consequently, the melancholy provoked by the English landscape garden was deeply selective: it mourned the mythical, distant fall of classical Greece and medieval Christendom while actively repressing the immediate, human devastation wrought by agrarian capitalism. In this light, the artificial ruin emerges not merely as a poignant testament to cosmic mortality, but as an elegant ideological screen that aestheticized dispossession and naturalized aristocratic privilege under the guise of philosophical contemplation.</p>",
