    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>Over the past three decades, street art has evolved from an illicit subterranean counterculture into one of the most fiercely contested battlegrounds of contemporary visual politics. Originating in the marginalized boroughs of Philadelphia and New York as competitive aerosol tagging, the medium reached a global theoretical inflection point through the unauthorized stencil interventions of anonymous British practitioner Banksy. Deploying quick-drying spray paint and pre-cut cardboard templates under cover of darkness, Banksy bypassed the exclusionary gatekeepers of the gallery system to transform the physical city into an open-air forum. In doing so, street art asserted what urban theorist Henri Lefebvre famously formulated as the &ldquo;right to the city&rdquo;&mdash;the radical claim that ordinary inhabitants, rather than state planners or corporate landowners, possess the fundamental entitlement to shape the visual and social fabric of their shared daily existence. Street art thus exposed a critical crisis: the relentless privatization, hyper-surveillance, and corporate colonization of public space.</p>\n<p>To understand the political stakes of street art, one must recognize how modern neoliberal metropolises manage visual culture. Urban citizens are ceaselessly bombarded by corporate billboards, illuminated transit advertisements, and sponsored digital displays that relentlessly solicit consumption without democratic consent. Yet when an unsanctioned artist paints a critical message onto a blank brick wall, the state immediately mobilizes punitive legal apparatuses to prosecute the intervention as criminal vandalism. Street art disrupts this structural asymmetry through what Guy Debord and the Situationist International termed <i>détournement</i>&mdash;the subversive rerouting and hijacking of dominant commercial imagery to expose its underlying ideological violence. By painting playful silhouettes of riot police searching toddlers, chimpanzees proclaiming their future dominion over parliament, or young girls floating away on heart-shaped balloons, Banksy reclaims the built environment from corporate hegemony, returning urban walls to their ancient role as democratic civic commons.</p>\n<p>However, the radical subversiveness of street art has run headlong into a profound structural paradox: the astonishing capacity of late-stage capitalism to commodify its own critique. In a process French sociologist Luc Boltanski identified as capitalist recuperation, the speculative art market systematically neutralizes insurgent gestures by converting them into astronomical financial assets. Auction houses routinely dispatch masonry contractors to saw Banksy murals directly out of distressed public housing estates, transporting crumbling cinder blocks into white-cube auction rooms where they sell for millions to hedge-fund billionaires. Simultaneously, commercial property developers actively exploit street art to curate an aura of edgy urban authenticity, utilizing colorful neighborhood murals to justify escalating rental rates. Municipal authorities that aggressively criminalize working-class graffiti writers suddenly install protective shatterproof Perspex sheets over Banksy stencils, rebranding neglected neighborhoods as lucrative tourist destinations and accelerating the gentrification that violently displaces longtime residents.</p>\n<p>This predatory absorption reached peak theatrical absurdity in 2018 during Sotheby&rsquo;s auction of Banksy&rsquo;s canvas <i>Girl with Balloon</i>. The instant the gavel struck at over one million British pounds, a secret shredder concealed within the ornate frame activated, slicing the canvas into hanging ribbons. Intended as a devastating prank to destroy the artwork and sabotage the auction&rsquo;s commercial ritual, the stunt backfired spectacularly. Retitled <i>Love is in the Bin</i>, the half-shredded relic was immediately declared a historic conceptual masterpiece, reselling three years later for an astonishing eighteen million pounds. By instantly transmuting an act of anti-capitalist sabotage into unprecedented speculative equity, the art market delivered a humbling object lesson: within the totalizing machinery of contemporary global capital, no act of urban rebellion is immune to being monetized, domesticated, and sold back to the ruling class.</p>",
