    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;36)</div>\n<p>For much of the nineteenth century, Western historiography operated under the shadow of Thomas Carlyle&rsquo;s famous dictum: &ldquo;The history of what man has accomplished in this world, is at bottom the History of the Great Men who have worked here.&rdquo; In Carlyle&rsquo;s heroic paradigm, historical epochs were forged by the sovereign will, charismatic genius, and decisive battlefield choices of exceptional political and military leaders. From Alexander to Napoleon, individuals were perceived as the primary prime movers of human destiny, steering civilizations through sheer moral resolve. Traditional university history departments institutionalized this ethos, obsessively mining state archives and diplomatic treaties to construct patriotic national mythologies. However, this biographical, narrative-driven framework was decisively dismantled in the twentieth century by the pioneering French Annales School. Founded by Marc Bloch and Lucien Febvre, and elevated to its theoretical zenith by Fernand Braudel, the Annales historians argued that traditional political history was merely a superficial chronicle that obscured the profound, subterranean forces governing human existence.</p>\n<p>Braudel&rsquo;s monumental 1949 masterpiece, <i>The Mediterranean and the Mediterranean World in the Age of Philip II</i>, systematically relegated great men and military triumphs to incidental epiphenomena. To achieve this, Braudel formulated a revolutionary tripartite architecture of historical time. At the highest, most ephemeral tier sits <i>l&rsquo;histoire &eacute;v&eacute;nementielle</i>&mdash;the history of surface events, court intrigues, and political treaties. Braudel famously dismissed this traditional chronicle as mere &ldquo;surface agitation, the crest of foam that the tides of history carry on their strong backs.&rdquo; Beneath these ephemeral ripples lies the intermediate stratum of <i>conjonctures</i>: cyclical economic fluctuations, demographic trends, and institutional trade shifts operating over decades. But grounding all human life is the deepest, most decisive temporal layer: <i>la longue dur&eacute;e</i>&mdash;a quasi-immobile, structural time measured in centuries, dictated by geographical topography, maritime currents, soil fertility, mountain barriers, and climate patterns.</p>\n<p>Through this geographical lens, the epic ideological conflicts of the sixteenth century appear radically altered. The Battle of Lepanto in 1571, long celebrated by European chroniclers as a heroic turning point where Christian naval valor halted Ottoman maritime expansion, is reconceptualized by Braudel as a fleeting tactical tremor with virtually no lasting structural impact. In Braudel&rsquo;s analysis, the enduring destiny of the Mediterranean basin was not determined by admiral Don John of Austria or Sultan Selim II, but by immutable environmental constraints: chronic timber shortages that restricted galley construction, seasonal Mediterranean wind currents that governed trade routes, and fragile agrarian yields that dictated municipal carrying capacity. History, Braudel insisted, is not enacted by heroic human intentions; rather, humans are prisoners of geographical and ecological structures that dictate the boundaries of possibility long before any battle is joined.</p>\n<p>Yet this profound shift away from individual agency has attracted serious criticism from contemporary historians. By reducing political choices and moral crises to negligible froth upon immutable geological waves, Braudelian structuralism risks descending into fatalistic determinism. If human beings are merely passive instruments of ecological forces and demographic rhythms, the concepts of ethical responsibility, individual courage, and insurgent resistance are effectively stripped of meaning. Critics note that while geography undoubtedly constrains human action, historical ruptures&mdash;from religious reformations to revolutionary uprisings&mdash;often erupt precisely when conscious human actors mobilize to challenge structural limits. Thus, while the Annales School permanently enriched historiography by exhuming the silent longue dur&eacute;e, the most compelling modern histories recognize that historical truth resides in the dialectical friction between structural constraints and the unpredictable spark of human agency.</p>",
