    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In the late 1970s, an innovative cohort of Italian historians mounted a decisive challenge to both traditional great-man biography and the prevailing macro-quantitative paradigms of structural social science. Known as microhistory (<i>microstoria</i>), this movement&mdash;pioneered by Carlo Ginzburg, Giovanni Levi, and Edoardo Grendi&mdash;argued that vast statistical models and aggregate economic curves treated human beings as faceless demographic abstractions. While macro-history could chart long-term price fluctuations or demographic collapses, it remained blind to the lived mentalities, agency, and subjective cosmologies of ordinary people. By radically reducing the scale of historical observation to a single village, a parish trial, or an obscure individual, microhistorians deployed what Grendi termed the &ldquo;exceptional normal&rdquo;&mdash;the principle that an eccentric archival anomaly, precisely because it runs afoul of established authority, can illuminate the tacit cultural norms and boundaries of a society far better than standard documentation. Intensive microscopic focus does not narrow historical understanding; rather, it reveals the intricate social fabric and internal contradictions that grand overarching theories inevitably smooth over.</p>\n<p>The definitive triumph of this methodology arrived in 1976 with Carlo Ginzburg&rsquo;s masterpiece, <i>The Cheese and the Worms</i>. Drawing upon meticulously preserved records from the Roman Inquisition spanning two decades of trials, Ginzburg reconstructed the extraordinary intellectual world of Domenico Scandella, known as Menocchio, a sixteenth-century miller from the remote Friulian village of Montereale. When brought before inquisitors for heresy, Menocchio articulated a startlingly original materialist cosmology: he asserted that the universe was born out of chaos just as cheese forms from milk, and that God and the angels emerged spontaneously from this primordial matter &ldquo;just as worms appear in cheese.&rdquo; Moreover, Menocchio boldly claimed that blaspheming against God was no worse than cheating one&rsquo;s neighbor and that all religions&mdash;Christian, Muslim, and Jewish&mdash;possessed equal dignity in the eyes of the creator.</p>\n<p>Through Menocchio&rsquo;s testimony, Ginzburg revolutionized how historians conceptualize subaltern culture. Prior to Ginzburg, cultural historians typically treated popular culture either as an uncritical, degraded imitation of elite ideas or as a pristine, isolated folk reservoir untainted by book culture. Ginzburg demonstrated instead a dynamic &ldquo;circularity&rdquo; between dominant and subordinate classes. Although Menocchio was a peasant, he was literate, devouring whatever vernacular books he could borrow, including the <i>Decameron</i> and travel chronicles of the Orient. Yet Menocchio did not passively absorb these printed texts; he aggressively filtered them through an ancient, oral peasant culture grounded in agrarian materialism and communal solidarity. In this circular dialogue, elite printed texts were creatively hijacked and repurposed to articulate radical subaltern resistance against ecclesiastical authority.</p>\n<p>Yet microhistory faces an inherent epistemological dilemma: the distortion of the archival record. Subaltern voices rarely entered written history voluntarily; they were documented almost exclusively when coercive state or ecclesiastical apparatuses interrogated, prosecuted, or punished them. Menocchio survives in the historical record solely because the Inquisition subjected him to agonizing interrogations, torture, and eventual burning at the stake. To recover the authentic peasant worldview, Ginzburg formulated what he termed the &ldquo;evidential paradigm&rdquo;&mdash;reading the archival transcript against the grain. Rather than accepting the inquisitors&rsquo; doctrinal framing, the microhistorian searches for slips of the tongue, moments of confusion, and persistent doctrinal recalcitrance where the defendant&rsquo;s genuine voice ruptures the legal script. Microhistory thus transforms the instruments of judicial repression into fragile monuments of subaltern defiance, proving that even within totalitarian systems, human consciousness retains the capacity to invent entirely new universes.</p>",
