    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 8 (Questions 43&ndash;48)</div>\n<p>Few philosophical debates within historiography are as intractable as the clash between historical determinism and counterfactual contingency. Are human events governed by inexorable macro-historical laws that render major outcomes inevitable, or is the course of civilization radically contingent&mdash;perched upon fragile, accidental hinges that could easily have swung in entirely different directions? In the seventeenth century, Blaise Pascal memorably articulated the contingency thesis in his <i>Pens&eacute;es</i> with the famous quip: &ldquo;Cleopatra&rsquo;s nose, had it been shorter, the whole face of the world would have been changed.&rdquo; Pascal implied that Mark Antony&rsquo;s infatuation with the Egyptian queen, influenced by a mere detail of facial anatomy, altered the fate of the Roman Republic and subsequent Western civilization. In sharp contrast, nineteenth-century deterministic systems, most famously articulated in Marxist historical materialism, posited that history is driven by teleological laws of economic development and class struggle, rendering individual idiosyncrasies mere froth upon the tide of inevitable historical stages.</p>\n<p>Throughout the twentieth century, academic historians largely viewed counterfactual speculation with deep suspicion. In his influential 1961 lectures <i>What Is History?</i>, British historian E.H. Carr dismissed counterfactual &ldquo;what-if&rdquo; scenarios as frivolous parlor games, arguing that the historian&rsquo;s sole professional obligation is to explain why events actually occurred, not to indulge in nostalgic daydreaming about what might have been. For Carr, entertaining counterfactuals was an intellectual indulgence practiced by defeated political factions seeking to comfort themselves with imaginary victories. Deterministic perspectives gained further credibility through sociological and structural models, which insisted that broad structural pressures&mdash;such as industrialization, imperial competition, and fiscal crises&mdash;exert such overwhelming gravitational force that individual accidents ultimately cancel each other out.</p>\n<p>However, the outbreak of catastrophic historical ruptures severely complicates deterministic assumptions. Consider the origins of World War I in the summer of 1914. Determinists argue that rigid alliance networks, naval arms races, and imperial rivalries made a general European conflagration completely inevitable. Yet a granular forensic examination of the Sarajevo crisis reveals an astonishing chain of contingent mishaps: the first assassin&rsquo;s bomb bounced off Archduke Franz Ferdinand&rsquo;s motorcar without harming him; the subsequent procession altered its planned route without informing the driver; the driver took an erroneous turn onto Franz Josef Street and stalled the engine while attempting to reverse; and assassin Gavrilo Princip happened to be standing outside a delicatessen only five feet away. Had the imperial chauffeur possessed a correct map, the assassination would have failed. While structural tensions created combustible geopolitical dry tinder, the specific, catastrophic spark was intensely contingent. Without that unique spark, the alliance treaties might well have decayed, peaceful diplomatic compromises could have been brokered, and the twentieth century might have unfolded without the horrors of mechanized trench warfare and the totalitarian regimes it spawned.</p>\n<p>In response to these dilemmas, contemporary historians have championed &ldquo;virtual history,&rdquo; deploying disciplined, plausible counterfactuals not as escapist fantasies, but as indispensable diagnostic tools to measure causal weight. As Niall Ferguson and other theorists argue, to claim that Factor A caused Event B is logically equivalent to asserting that without Factor A, Event B would not have occurred. Therefore, testing plausible alternative paths is essential for establishing causal necessity. Determinism, by treating the actual outcome as the only possible reality, falls into the teleological fallacy of hindsight bias. Conversely, unconstrained contingency degenerates into pure chaos, ignoring how deep economic and geopolitical structures sharply delimit the horizon of possible outcomes. A mature historical methodology must navigate between these poles, recognizing that while deep structural currents channel the river of time, contingent human choices and chaotic accidents determine which banks it will burst.</p>",
